nced-embed-3","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#pinned-settings","content":"Pinned settings","locale":"en"},"6141":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-4","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"Address, port and access token","locale":"en"},"6142":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-5","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#tunnels","content":"Tunnels and reverse proxies","locale":"en"},"6143":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-6","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#path-prefix","content":"Path prefix","locale":"en"},"6144":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-7","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#mounting-into-an-existing-fastapi-app","content":"Mounting into an existing FastAPI app","locale":"en"},"6145":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-8","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#calling-the-services-directly","content":"Calling the services directly","locale":"en"},"6146":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-9","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","content":"RVC Next can be started as a library from another Python program. The server it starts is the same as `rvc-next webui`: the same API, the same web UI, the same jobs and live voice changing. The host program decides where the data goes, where the settings file goes, which settings the user may not change, and which path the UI sits under.","locale":"en"},"6147":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-10","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","content":"The host program must run in the same Python environment as RVC Next, and that environment must meet RVC Next's requirements (see Installation). Do not install it into a shared environment with conflicting dependencies.","locale":"en"},"6148":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-11","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#a-minimal-example","content":"`start()` runs the server on a background thread and returns the web UI's address; `run()` serves in the foreground until `Ctrl+C`. The object is also a context manager:","locale":"en"},"6149":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-12","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#a-minimal-example","content":"`stop()` may be called more than once; it closes the server, the live voice changing process and the database, and frees the port. `import rvc_next` loads neither torch nor FastAPI; the server is loaded only when `RvcNextServer` is used.","locale":"en"},"6150":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-13","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#data-folder-and-configuration-folder","content":"Parameter","locale":"en"},"6151":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-14","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#data-folder-and-configuration-folder","content":"What it holds","locale":"en"},"6152":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-15","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#data-folder-and-configuration-folder","content":"Default","locale":"en"},"6153":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-16","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#data-folder-and-configuration-folder","content":"`data_dir`","locale":"en"},"6154":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-17","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#data-folder-and-configuration-folder","content":"The database, models, experiments, results, uploads, caches, and `server.json`","locale":"en"},"6155":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-18","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#data-folder-and-configuration-folder","content":"`RVC_NEXT_DATA_DIR` or the system default","locale":"en"},"6156":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-19","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#data-folder-and-configuration-folder","content":"`config_dir`","locale":"en"},"6157":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-20","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#data-folder-and-configuration-folder","content":"The settings file, `settings.toml`","locale":"en"},"6158":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-21","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#data-folder-and-configuration-folder","content":"Same as `data_dir`","locale":"en"},"6159":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-22","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#data-folder-and-configuration-folder","content":"`settings_path`","locale":"en"},"6160":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-23","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#data-folder-and-configuration-folder","content":"The settings file's full path (any file name); takes precedence over `config_dir`","locale":"en"},"6161":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-24","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#data-folder-and-configuration-folder","content":"None","locale":"en"},"6162":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-25","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#data-folder-and-configuration-folder","content":"A host program usually has its own configuration folder; RVC Next's settings can go there while the data stays in the data folder:","locale":"en"},"6163":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-26","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#data-folder-and-configuration-folder","content":"**Settings › About** in the web UI shows the data folder and the settings file in use. The command line supports the same: `rvc-next webui --config-dir <folder>`, or the environment variable `RVC_NEXT_CONFIG_DIR` (the easiest way to make `rvc-next config` and the other commands read and write the same settings).","locale":"en"},"6164":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-27","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#pinned-settings","content":"`settings` takes a dictionary shaped like `settings.toml`. These values sit above the settings file and the environment, are **never written to the file**, and cannot be changed through the web UI or the API — for what the host program manages itself:","locale":"en"},"6165":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-28","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#pinned-settings","content":"Pinned folders are marked **Set by the application rvc-next runs in** under **Settings › Storage**. Every setting is listed in Configuration and environment variables.","locale":"en"},"6166":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-29","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"Parameter","locale":"en"},"6167":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-30","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"Description","locale":"en"},"6168":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-31","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"`host`","locale":"en"},"6169":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-32","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"Address to bind; defaults to the `server.host` setting (`127.0.0.1`)","locale":"en"},"6170":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-33","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"`port`","locale":"en"},"6171":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-34","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"`0` lets the system pick a free port; a number is the first port to try (moving up when taken, at most 20); `None` uses the `server.port` setting","locale":"en"},"6172":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-35","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"`strict_port`","locale":"en"},"6173":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-36","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"When `True`, a taken port is an error","locale":"en"},"6174":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-37","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"`access_token`","locale":"en"},"6175":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-38","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"An optional access token; once set, every API and socket request must carry it (the web UI asks for it once). Not required for a non-local address or `extra_hosts` either","locale":"en"},"6176":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-39","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"`extra_hosts`","locale":"en"},"6177":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-40","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"Public host names accepted besides the bound address, such as a tunnel's or a reverse proxy's; see Tunnels and reverse proxies below","locale":"en"},"6178":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-41","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"`open_browser`","locale":"en"},"6179":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-42","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"Whether to open a browser after starting; `False` by default","locale":"en"},"6180":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-43","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"`log_level`","locale":"en"},"6181":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-44","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"uvicorn's log level; `\"warning\"` by default","locale":"en"},"6182":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-45","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#address-port-and-access-token","content":"`host`, `access_token` and `open_browser` also take effect as pinned settings. For remote access, see Remote access and security.","locale":"en"},"6183":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-46","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#tunnels","content":"When listening only locally, the server accepts only requests whose `Host` is a local name or the bound address. A tunnel (such as a Gradio share link) or a reverse proxy forwards its own public host name, which has to be allowed first. A tunnel's address is often known only once the server has started and its port is fixed, so `allow_host()` can be called after `start()` too and takes effect at once:","locale":"en"},"6184":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-47","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#tunnels","content":"Anyone with the public address can reach the server and use every feature; pass `access_token` if you want one, or add authentication on the host application's or the proxy's side. Requests arriving through these names are not treated as local, even though the tunnel connects from the same computer, so they cannot use **Show in folder**.","locale":"en"},"6185":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-48","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#path-prefix","content":"`api_prefix` moves the API, the socket and the web UI under one path, so they cannot collide with the host program's own routes:","locale":"en"},"6186":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-49","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#path-prefix","content":"The web UI needs no rebuild: it derives its base path from the address of its own script. The command line supports the same with `rvc-next webui --api-prefix /voice`.","locale":"en"},"6187":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-50","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#mounting-into-an-existing-fastapi-app","content":"When the host program is itself a FastAPI (or any ASGI) application, it can mount RVC Next instead of opening another port:","locale":"en"},"6188":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-51","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#mounting-into-an-existing-fastapi-app","content":"The host enters the child app's `lifespan` on its own event loop (it starts forwarding events to the socket) and calls `services.close()` when it exits. `start_background=True` starts the job scheduler and idle unloading; without it, queued jobs do not run.","locale":"en"},"6189":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-52","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#calling-the-services-directly","content":"`server.services` is the same set of service objects the server uses; the host program can call them without HTTP, for example to list voices, submit a conversion or change settings:","locale":"en"},"6190":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed-53","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-embed#calling-the-services-directly","content":"The methods match the API one to one and return models shaped like the API's JSON; see Core services.","locale":"en"},"6191":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","type":"page","content":"FAQ","breadcrumbs":["RVC Next Documentation","Advanced"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","locale":"en"},"6192":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-0","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","content":"Common questions grouped by topic. Expand a question to see its answer. If you encounter a problem, run rvc-next doctor first: it checks torch and CUDA, audio devices, and assets, and suggests commands to fix issues.","locale":"en"},"6193":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-1","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#installation-and-startup","content":"Installation and startup","locale":"en"},"6194":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-2","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#asset-and-model-downloads","content":"Asset and model downloads","locale":"en"},"6195":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-3","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#conversion","content":"Conversion","locale":"en"},"6196":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-4","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"Live voice conversion","locale":"en"},"6197":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-5","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#gpu-memory-and-performance","content":"GPU memory and performance","locale":"en"},"6198":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-6","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#files-and-remote-access","content":"Files and remote access","locale":"en"},"6199":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-7","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#installation-and-startup","content":"The default port is 7868. If it is in use, the server automatically tries 7869, 7870, … (up to 20 ports), logs \"Port 7868 is unavailable. Using port 7869.\", and opens the browser on the actual port.","locale":"en"},"6200":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-8","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#installation-and-startup","content":"To use a specific port:","locale":"en"},"6201":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-9","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#installation-and-startup","content":"With `--strict-port` (or `server.strict_port` set to `true`), the server fails immediately if the port is in use (exit code 3), without switching ports.","locale":"en"},"6202":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-10","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#installation-and-startup","content":"By default, in the data directory: `%APPDATA%\\rvc-next` on Windows, `~/Library/Application Support/rvc-next` on macOS, and `~/.local/share/rvc-next` (or `$XDG_DATA_HOME/rvc-next`) on Linux. `data_dir` in `rvc-next config show`, or **Data folder** under **Settings** › **About** in the Web UI, shows the current location.","locale":"en"},"6203":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-11","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#installation-and-startup","content":"Use `RVC_NEXT_DATA_DIR` or `rvc-next webui --data-dir <path>` to choose another location. To move only one category of files, change the **Voices**, **Experiments**, **Results**, or **Assets** folder under **Settings** › **Storage**. For the directory layout, see Configuration and environment variables.","locale":"en"},"6204":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-12","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#installation-and-startup","content":"The data directory can be moved, renamed or copied as a whole (with rvc-next stopped): start rvc-next with the new location and results, uploads, voices and experiments are found there, and finished training stages stay finished. A result whose file is gone is shown as missing; `rvc-next output check` lists every missing file, and `--prune-missing` removes their records.","locale":"en"},"6205":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-13","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#installation-and-startup","content":"Stop the server, then delete or rename `settings.toml` in the data directory (`rvc-next config path` prints its path). The next start uses all defaults; the voice library, experiments, and results are unaffected.","locale":"en"},"6206":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-14","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#installation-and-startup","content":"To reset just one setting, write it back with `rvc-next config set <key> <default value>`. Defaults are listed in Configuration and environment variables. Values set by `RVC_NEXT_<GROUP>__<FIELD>` environment variables always take precedence.","locale":"en"},"6207":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-15","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#installation-and-startup","content":"The server picks up `settings.toml` changes within about 1 second, and the Web UI refreshes too. If nothing changes, first check the server log for an “Ignored a change to … settings.toml” warning (an invalid file edit). Then check whether an environment variable overrides the same setting (`rvc-next config show` marks this). A few settings, such as the port and listening address, require a server restart.","locale":"en"},"6208":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-16","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#installation-and-startup","content":"Yes. Command-line jobs run in their own processes and write records to the same database, so both the Web UI's **Jobs** panel and `rvc-next jobs list` show jobs from either side. Keep two points in mind: command-line jobs do not participate in the server's GPU queue, so concurrent jobs compete for GPU memory; for settings changes, see the previous question.","locale":"en"},"6209":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-17","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#asset-and-model-downloads","content":"Conversion needs at least HuBERT, and also RMVPE when using the RMVPE pitch method. When assets are missing, the UI shows **Needs …** and a **Download (size)** button; click it to download. You can also download them in **Models** › **Assets**. Command-line errors suggest the specific command (exit code 7), for example:","locale":"en"},"6210":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-18","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#asset-and-model-downloads","content":"If you have original RVC, point `paths.assets_dir` to its `assets` folder to avoid downloading again; see Migrate from original RVC.","locale":"en"},"6211":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-19","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#asset-and-model-downloads","content":"The FCPE model (`fcpe`, about 41 MB) is an asset in the default `inference` download group, so `rvc-next assets download` with no arguments fetches it too. If only FCPE is missing, the UI prompts you to download it when you select FCPE; on the command line you can also run `rvc-next assets download fcpe` on its own.","locale":"en"},"6212":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-20","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#asset-and-model-downloads","content":"Switch to ModelScope or the hf-mirror endpoint:","locale":"en"},"6213":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-21","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#asset-and-model-downloads","content":"Under **Settings** › **Downloads** › **Download source**, select **ModelScope** or **hf-mirror.com**. Or select **Custom** and enter another mirror in **Custom endpoint**.","locale":"en"},"6214":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-22","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#asset-and-model-downloads","content":"Downloads support resuming: after an interruption, downloading again continues from the `.part` file, with automatic retries on failure. **Model repository** can switch between `rvc-model` (default, including demo voices) and the official repository; both contain the same model files. ModelScope has only rvc-model's copy, so the community base models and Applio's content-feature models still come from Hugging Face; a file the chosen source lacks or serves damaged comes from another source automatically.","locale":"en"},"6215":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-23","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#asset-and-model-downloads","content":"The file's SHA-256 differs from the catalog, usually because the download is incomplete or the file was replaced. Download the asset again in **Models** › **Assets**, or run `rvc-next assets download <id>`. `rvc-next assets verify` rechecks all installed assets.","locale":"en"},"6216":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-24","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#conversion","content":"First check whether the GPU is actually being used: the \"Selected\" line in `rvc-next doctor` shows the selected device. Common reasons for seeing CPU:","locale":"en"},"6217":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-25","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#conversion","content":"You installed a CPU build of PyTorch and need a CUDA build (see Installation);","locale":"en"},"6218":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-26","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#conversion","content":"The GPU does not meet the GPU rules (less than 4 GiB of memory or insufficient compute capability); see Hardware and performance;","locale":"en"},"6219":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-27","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#conversion","content":"`compute.device` is set to `cpu`.","locale":"en"},"6220":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-28","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#conversion","content":"If CPU is your only option, switch the pitch method to **pm (fast)** and use **Preview 15 s** to tune parameters before converting the whole song. Vocal separation is especially slow on CPU, so skip it if you can.","locale":"en"},"6221":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-29","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#conversion","content":"Without an index, **Index strength** has no effect (the UI tells you). Conversion still works, but voice characteristics come entirely from the model itself. To attach an index:","locale":"en"},"6222":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-30","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#conversion","content":"Click **Attach index** on the voice page and upload or choose a server `.index` file (any file name is accepted, but the version must match: 256 dimensions for v1, 768 for v2);","locale":"en"},"6223":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-31","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#conversion","content":"If you have a training experiment, click **Build from experiment**;","locale":"en"},"6224":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-32","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#conversion","content":"Command line: `rvc-next model index attach <voice> <file>`, or `rvc-next model index build <voice> --experiment <experiment>`.","locale":"en"},"6225":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-33","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#conversion","content":"`trained_*.index` contains no feature vectors and cannot be used; use `added_*.index`.","locale":"en"},"6226":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-34","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#conversion","content":"A voice with speaker information in its `.pth` is multi-speaker. Its card shows **N speakers**, and the parameter panel includes a **Speaker** option. On the command line, use `--speaker <id>`; `rvc-next model info <voice> --speakers` lists speakers. You can name them with `rvc-next model edit <voice> --speaker-name 0=Alice`. Multi-speaker voices can also have a separate index for each speaker (`--speaker`). Live voice conversion uses the selected speaker too.","locale":"en"},"6227":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-35","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#conversion","content":"By default, in `outputs/<date>/<job id>/` under the data directory, with file names `<original name>.<voice>.<format>`. Existing files are never overwritten. The Web UI's **Results** list lets you play or download them (**Download all** packages them as a zip), or click **Show in folder** (only in a browser on the server machine). On the command line, use `-o` to specify an output folder; each result's path is printed when finished.","locale":"en"},"6228":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-36","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#conversion","content":"To change the default location, set the **Results** folder under **Settings** › **Storage**. When **Delete results after days (0: keep)** is greater than 0, the server deletes expired results at startup.","locale":"en"},"6229":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-37","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#conversion","content":"If an imported index cannot be paired with certainty (for example, several voices of the same version are imported together and their names do not match), the Web UI opens **Review the import**. Each index lists only version-compatible voices for you to choose. You can also select **Keep unassigned** and assign it later under **Unassigned indexes** in **Models** › **Voices**. The command line prints the import plan and exits with code 3; rerun with `--pair voice.pth=index.index` or `--keep-unassigned`.","locale":"en"},"6230":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-38","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"Live voice conversion accesses sound cards through PortAudio. On Linux, install the system PortAudio library:","locale":"en"},"6231":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-39","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"Then check the \"Audio devices\" line in `rvc-next doctor`, or list devices with `rvc-next live devices`. **Live is not available on this server yet.** means `sounddevice` is not installed in the server's Python environment.","locale":"en"},"6232":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-40","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"Also remember: these are devices on **the server computer**. Opening the Web UI from another computer or phone does not show or use that device's microphone; see Remote access and security.","locale":"en"},"6233":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-41","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"Audio cuts out when inference cannot keep up with the block length. Check **Load** on the Live page: above 80%, **Increase block to … ms** appears; click it to apply. You can also increase **Block** manually, or:","locale":"en"},"6234":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-42","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"Reduce **Context**, disable **Reduce input noise** and **Reduce output noise**;","locale":"en"},"6235":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-43","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"Switch the pitch method to pm;","locale":"en"},"6236":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-44","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"Stop concurrent conversion or training jobs (they compete with live voice conversion for the GPU);","locale":"en"},"6237":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-45","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"Confirm the GPU is actually being used (CPU usually needs very long blocks).","locale":"en"},"6238":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-46","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"Continually increasing **underruns** and **overruns** counts also indicate a need for larger blocks. See Hardware and performance.","locale":"en"},"6239":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-47","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"The output device used last time is disconnected (for example, headphones or a sound card were unplugged). The UI reports **The saved output device is missing; the system default would be used.** Click **Start with the default output** to confirm, or choose another device in the device panel. On the command line, add `--allow-fallback`. With **Reconnect a lost device automatically** enabled, a device lost during a session resumes automatically when reconnected.","locale":"en"},"6240":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-48","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"Send Live's output into a virtual audio device and choose that device's recording side as the microphone in the other program: **CABLE Input** → **CABLE Output** with VB-CABLE or **Voicemeeter AUX Input** → **Voicemeeter Out B1** with Voicemeeter (Windows), **BlackHole 2ch** (macOS), or a PipeWire/PulseAudio virtual microphone (Linux). Use **Monitor** to hear yourself. Step by step, with the settings to turn off in the other program, in Use Live in other apps.","locale":"en"},"6241":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-49","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"The other program must record the virtual device's *recording* side (**CABLE Output**, **Voicemeeter Out B1**, **BlackHole 2ch**, **RVC-Next-Microphone**), not **Default** and not your real microphone.","locale":"en"},"6242":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-50","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"rvc-next's **Output** must be the *playback* side, and Live must be **Running**.","locale":"en"},"6243":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-51","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"With Voicemeeter, the strip rvc-next plays into needs **B1** on, and the microphone's own strip **B1** off.","locale":"en"},"6244":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-52","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"On Linux, the virtual sink is not a device of its own in rvc-next's menus: add the **rvc-next-virtual** ALSA device described in Use Live in other apps › Linux.","locale":"en"},"6245":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-53","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#live-voice-conversion","content":"See the troubleshooting table at the end of Use Live in other apps.","locale":"en"},"6246":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-54","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#gpu-memory-and-performance","content":"An out-of-memory failure is not retried automatically; the server unloads all unused models. You can:","locale":"en"},"6247":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-55","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#gpu-memory-and-performance","content":"Stop live voice conversion and other jobs, then click **Free GPU memory** in the top bar's **GPU memory** menu;","locale":"en"},"6248":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-56","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#gpu-memory-and-performance","content":"Click **Retry** in **Jobs**;","locale":"en"},"6249":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-57","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#gpu-memory-and-performance","content":"If memory is still insufficient, set **GPU jobs at once** to 1, reduce the training batch size, disable **Cache the dataset in GPU memory**, or set **Precision** to fp16 (if the GPU supports it).","locale":"en"},"6250":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-58","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#gpu-memory-and-performance","content":"See Hardware and performance.","locale":"en"},"6251":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-59","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#gpu-memory-and-performance","content":"When the number of GPU jobs reaches **GPU jobs at once**, other jobs queue. While live voice conversion runs, new GPU jobs also wait to avoid affecting live audio. **Run anyway** on the job card starts a job without waiting for live voice conversion to stop (which may cause audio glitches).","locale":"en"},"6252":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-60","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#gpu-memory-and-performance","content":"Jobs still queued or running when the server exits are marked **Interrupted** at the next start. Their requests are saved in the database; click **Retry** to rerun. Training resumes from the last saved checkpoint.","locale":"en"},"6253":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-61","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#files-and-remote-access","content":"By default, **Server files** can browse all drives (`C:\\`, `D:\\`, etc. on Windows), mounted volumes (`/Volumes/*` on macOS; `/media/<user>/*`, `/run/media/<user>/*`, `/mnt/*` on Linux), the user's home directory, the data directory, and original RVC installations.","locale":"en"},"6254":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-62","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#files-and-remote-access","content":"If a drive is missing, **Folders the browser may open** (`paths.browse_roots`) is usually set: once set, only listed folders can be browsed. Add the drive you need, or clear the list to restore defaults:","locale":"en"},"6255":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-63","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#files-and-remote-access","content":"The command line is not subject to this restriction.","locale":"en"},"6256":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-64","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#files-and-remote-access","content":"Bind to all interfaces:","locale":"en"},"6257":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-65","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#files-and-remote-access","content":"On the other computer, open `http://<server IP>:7868`; no access token is needed. If the network is not fully trusted, add a firewall, a VPN or a reverse proxy with authentication, or set the optional access token (`rvc-next config set server.access_token \"a long random string\"`), after which the browser asks for it in the **Access token** dialog. See Remote access and security.","locale":"en"},"6258":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-66","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#files-and-remote-access","content":"The token is not displayed in plaintext (`config show` only reports whether it is set). Stop the server on the server computer, then set a new token or clear it:","locale":"en"},"6259":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-67","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#files-and-remote-access","content":"Once a token is set, all browsers (including those on the server machine) require it, so a forgotten token can only be reset from the command line. The browser shows the **Access token** dialog; enter the new token.","locale":"en"},"6260":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-68","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#files-and-remote-access","content":"Yes. In **Models** › **Voices**, use **Add an RVC install** under **Original RVC installs**. Voices in the original `assets/weights` appear directly in the voice list (marked **legacy**), with indexes recommended using the original rules. Point `paths.assets_dir` to the original `assets` folder to reuse HuBERT, RMVPE, base models, and separation models. See Migrate from original RVC.","locale":"en"},"6261":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq-69","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-faq#files-and-remote-access","content":"Library voices are moved to the system recycle bin (or `trash/` in the data directory if no recycle bin is available), where you can recover them. Voices in original RVC installations are only hidden; their files are unchanged. Enable **Show hidden** in the voice list, or use `rvc-next model list --all` to see them.","locale":"en"},"6262":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"page","content":"Hardware and performance","breadcrumbs":["RVC Next Documentation","Advanced"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","locale":"en"},"6263":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-0","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","content":"This page explains how RVC Next selects compute devices and precision, manages GPU memory, and which settings affect speed and live latency.","locale":"en"},"6264":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-1","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#select-a-device","content":"Select a device","locale":"en"},"6265":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-2","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-rule","content":"GPU rules","locale":"en"},"6266":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-3","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#precision","content":"Precision","locale":"en"},"6267":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-4","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#install-the-appropriate-pytorch","content":"Install the appropriate PyTorch","locale":"en"},"6268":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-5","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#cuda-graph","content":"CUDA Graph","locale":"en"},"6269":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-6","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#amd-rocm-and-intel-xpu","content":"AMD ROCm and Intel XPU","locale":"en"},"6270":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-7","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#directml","content":"DirectML","locale":"en"},"6271":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-8","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#apple-silicon-mps","content":"Apple Silicon (MPS)","locale":"en"},"6272":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-9","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-memory-management","content":"GPU memory management","locale":"en"},"6273":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-10","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#model-cache","content":"Model cache","locale":"en"},"6274":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-11","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#idle-unloading","content":"Idle unloading","locale":"en"},"6275":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-12","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#free-gpu-memory","content":"Free GPU memory","locale":"en"},"6276":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-13","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#out-of-memory","content":"Out of memory","locale":"en"},"6277":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-14","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-job-queues","content":"GPU job queues","locale":"en"},"6278":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-15","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#live-voice-conversion-has-priority","content":"Live voice conversion has priority","locale":"en"},"6279":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-16","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#live-latency","content":"Live voice conversion latency and block length","locale":"en"},"6280":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-17","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","content":"This page explains how RVC Next selects compute devices and precision, manages GPU memory, and which settings affect speed and live latency. Related settings are in the `compute` group (**Settings** › **Compute** in the Web UI). For the full list, see Configuration and environment variables.","locale":"en"},"6281":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-18","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#select-a-device","content":"`compute.device` (**Settings** › **Compute** › **Device**) accepts:","locale":"en"},"6282":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-19","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#select-a-device","content":"Value","locale":"en"},"6283":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-20","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#select-a-device","content":"Meaning","locale":"en"},"6284":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-21","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#select-a-device","content":"`auto` (default)","locale":"en"},"6285":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-22","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#select-a-device","content":"Selects the most suitable GPU (CUDA, ROCm or XPU) using the rules below. If none is available, tries DirectML, then CPU. `auto` does not select MPS","locale":"en"},"6286":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-23","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#select-a-device","content":"`cpu`","locale":"en"},"6287":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-24","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#select-a-device","content":"CPU only","locale":"en"},"6288":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-25","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#select-a-device","content":"`cuda:N`","locale":"en"},"6289":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-26","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#select-a-device","content":"NVIDIA GPU N (numbered from 0), or AMD GPU N on a ROCm build of torch. Also uses cards the GPU rules would skip (in fp32); falls back to CPU if the ID does not exist","locale":"en"},"6290":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-27","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#select-a-device","content":"`xpu:N`","locale":"en"},"6291":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-28","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#select-a-device","content":"Intel GPU N (Arc, Data Center GPU, recent integrated graphics) on an XPU build of torch. Same fallbacks as `cuda:N`","locale":"en"},"6292":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-29","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#select-a-device","content":"`dml`","locale":"en"},"6293":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-30","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#select-a-device","content":"DirectML (AMD, Intel, and other GPUs on Windows). Falls back to CPU if unavailable","locale":"en"},"6294":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-31","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#select-a-device","content":"`mps`","locale":"en"},"6295":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-32","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#select-a-device","content":"Metal on Apple Silicon (**experimental**). Falls back to CPU if unavailable","locale":"en"},"6296":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-33","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-rule","content":"`auto` follows the original RVC rules (`configs/config.py`), checking each CUDA GPU:","locale":"en"},"6297":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-34","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-rule","content":"GPU","locale":"en"},"6298":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-35","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-rule","content":"Result","locale":"en"},"6299":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-36","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-rule","content":"Less than 4 GiB of memory, or compute capability below 5.3","locale":"en"},"6300":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-37","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-rule","content":"Not used","locale":"en"},"6301":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-38","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-rule","content":"Compute capability 6.1 (Pascal, e.g. GTX 10 series), or GTX 16xx","locale":"en"},"6302":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-39","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-rule","content":"Used, fp32","locale":"en"},"6303":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-40","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-rule","content":"Compute capability above 6.1 (RTX 20 series and newer)","locale":"en"},"6304":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-41","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-rule","content":"Used, fp16","locale":"en"},"6305":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-42","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-rule","content":"Others (compute capability between 5.3 and 6.1)","locale":"en"},"6306":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-43","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-rule","content":"Not used","locale":"en"},"6307":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-44","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-rule","content":"AMD cards on ROCm (still `cuda:N`) and Intel XPU cards skip the compute capability rows: a card with at least 4 GiB of memory is used; ROCm cards run fp16, and XPU cards run fp16 when the device reports fp16 support.","locale":"en"},"6308":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-45","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-rule","content":"If several GPUs qualify, the one with the highest compute capability is selected; ties are resolved by the largest memory size. `rvc-next doctor` and the device list under **Settings** › **Compute** show each device, the precision assigned by the rules, and the reason (e.g. \"Pascal SM 6.1 and GTX 16xx cards run in fp32\").","locale":"en"},"6309":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-46","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#precision","content":"`compute.precision` (**Precision**):","locale":"en"},"6310":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-47","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#precision","content":"`auto`: follows the GPU rules;","locale":"en"},"6311":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-48","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#precision","content":"`fp32`: always uses fp32;","locale":"en"},"6312":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-49","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#precision","content":"`fp16`: forces fp16 on CUDA, ROCm and XPU (including GPUs assigned fp32 by the rules).","locale":"en"},"6313":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-50","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#precision","content":"DirectML, MPS, and CPU always use fp32 (except vocal separation on DirectML; see below). fp16 is faster and uses less GPU memory; if a card produces abnormal output in fp16, switch to `fp32`.","locale":"en"},"6314":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-51","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#precision","content":"Precision also determines chunk sizes for long audio (as in the original): CUDA, ROCm and XPU cards with at most 4 GiB of memory use the smallest chunks, fp16 uses larger chunks, and other cases use medium chunks.","locale":"en"},"6315":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-52","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#install-the-appropriate-pytorch","content":"Device availability depends on the installed PyTorch: NVIDIA GPUs need a CUDA build of torch, AMD GPUs a ROCm build (AMD's own, on Windows and Linux), Intel GPUs an XPU build. With only a CPU build installed, `auto` selects CPU. For installation instructions, see Installation; `rvc-next doctor` shows the torch version and what it was built for (for example `CUDA 13.0`, `ROCm 10.0` or `XPU`).","locale":"en"},"6316":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-53","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#cuda-graph","content":"CUDA Graph records an inference run and replays it directly afterward, reducing CPU scheduling overhead. It helps most with small blocks in live voice conversion:","locale":"en"},"6317":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-54","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#cuda-graph","content":"Setting","locale":"en"},"6318":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-55","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#cuda-graph","content":"UI label","locale":"en"},"6319":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-56","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#cuda-graph","content":"Default","locale":"en"},"6320":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-57","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#cuda-graph","content":"`compute.cuda_graph_offline`","locale":"en"},"6321":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-58","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#cuda-graph","content":"**CUDA Graph for conversion**","locale":"en"},"6322":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-59","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#cuda-graph","content":"Off","locale":"en"},"6323":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-60","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#cuda-graph","content":"`compute.cuda_graph_live`","locale":"en"},"6324":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-61","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#cuda-graph","content":"**CUDA Graph for Live**","locale":"en"},"6325":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-62","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#cuda-graph","content":"On","locale":"en"},"6326":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-63","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#cuda-graph","content":"It works only on NVIDIA CUDA: on ROCm and XPU the switches have no effect and inference runs normally. Recordings are cached by input shape (up to 8 per call site, adjustable with `RVC_NEXT_CUDA_GRAPH_MAX_CACHE`). If recording fails, inference automatically falls back to normal mode. Offline inputs vary in length, making recordings hard to reuse, so it is disabled by default.","locale":"en"},"6327":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-64","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#amd-rocm-and-intel-xpu","content":"ROCm: torch's ROCm build (Windows and Linux) presents AMD GPUs as CUDA devices, so they appear as `cuda:N` and everything that runs on CUDA runs on them, except CUDA Graph. Vocal separation uses the GPU directly.","locale":"en"},"6328":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-65","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#amd-rocm-and-intel-xpu","content":"XPU: Intel GPUs appear as `xpu:N`. Conversion, Live, training (fp16 with AMP; several cards train with the `xccl` backend) and pitch and feature extraction run on them. Vocal separation loads the model on the CPU, moves it to the GPU and runs in fp32, because pymss has no XPU support of its own.","locale":"en"},"6329":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-66","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#amd-rocm-and-intel-xpu","content":"Multi-GPU training selects cards with `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` (also read by ROCm) or, on XPU, `ZE_AFFINITY_MASK`. The GPU ids under **Train** refer to the devices of whichever of the two your torch build provides.","locale":"en"},"6330":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-67","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#directml","content":"Install DirectML support: `pip install \"rvc-next[directml]\"` (installs `torch-directml` and `onnxruntime-directml`).","locale":"en"},"6331":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-68","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#directml","content":"RMVPE uses its ONNX version on DirectML and needs the `rmvpe-onnx` asset: `rvc-next assets download rmvpe-onnx`. It is not in the `all` group; the UI and command line prompt you to download it if missing.","locale":"en"},"6332":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-69","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#directml","content":"Vocal separation runs in fp16 by default on DirectML, with smaller chunks. If fp16 fails, the job automatically reruns once in fp32 (the log includes \"Retrying in fp32\").","locale":"en"},"6333":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-70","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#apple-silicon-mps","content":"Set `compute.device` to `mps` to use Metal; `auto` does not select it. MPS runs in fp32 and is experimental.","locale":"en"},"6334":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-71","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#model-cache","content":"Models loaded by the server remain in memory (GPU memory), so they need not be reloaded next time: HuBERT, the various pitch models, and the last 3 voices and their indexes. Live voice conversion keeps its own copy in its worker process.","locale":"en"},"6335":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-72","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#model-cache","content":"The top bar's **GPU memory** indicator shows used/total GPU memory and the **Loaded:** model list (or **Nothing loaded** if empty).","locale":"en"},"6336":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-73","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#idle-unloading","content":"`compute.unload_after_minutes` (**Unload models after idle minutes (0: never)**, default: 10): models in the server are automatically unloaded after this many idle minutes. The server checks every 30 seconds.","locale":"en"},"6337":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-74","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#free-gpu-memory","content":"Click **Free GPU memory** in the top bar's **GPU memory** menu to immediately unload all cached models, including models in an idle live worker, and return GPU memory to the driver.","locale":"en"},"6338":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-75","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#free-gpu-memory","content":"Memory can be freed only when **no jobs are queued or running and live voice conversion is stopped**. Otherwise, the menu item reads **Free GPU memory (wait: a job is running)** or **Free GPU memory (wait: Live is running)**, because these models are in use and would need to be reloaded immediately.","locale":"en"},"6339":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-76","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#free-gpu-memory","content":"The command line has no separate release command: each command's process exits when finished, releasing its GPU memory.","locale":"en"},"6340":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-77","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#out-of-memory","content":"When inference or training reports an out-of-memory error (CUDA `OutOfMemoryError` or the corresponding MPS or DirectML errors):","locale":"en"},"6341":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-78","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#out-of-memory","content":"The job fails with **The GPU is not available or ran out of memory** (`compute_unavailable`, with `detail.reason` set to `out_of_memory`; command-line exit code 9);","locale":"en"},"6342":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-79","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#out-of-memory","content":"The server immediately unloads all unused models so the next run starts with empty GPU memory; if the live worker fails, it unloads its own models first;","locale":"en"},"6343":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-80","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#out-of-memory","content":"These failed jobs **are not retried automatically**. Click **Retry** in **Jobs** yourself.","locale":"en"},"6344":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-81","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#out-of-memory","content":"Ways to reduce GPU memory use:","locale":"en"},"6345":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-82","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#out-of-memory","content":"Keep **GPU jobs at once** at 1;","locale":"en"},"6346":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-83","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#out-of-memory","content":"**Free GPU memory** before conversion or training, or stop live voice conversion;","locale":"en"},"6347":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-84","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#out-of-memory","content":"Reduce the training batch size (`train run --batch-size`) and disable **Cache the dataset in GPU memory**;","locale":"en"},"6348":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-85","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#out-of-memory","content":"Switch to fp16 (if supported by the GPU), or use CPU on cards with very little memory.","locale":"en"},"6349":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-86","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-job-queues","content":"Conversion, separation, and training jobs are GPU jobs when `compute.device` is not `cpu`:","locale":"en"},"6350":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-87","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-job-queues","content":"`compute.gpu_jobs` (**GPU jobs at once**, default: 1, maximum: 16) limits concurrent GPU jobs. Others queue (state **Waiting**, **Waiting for the GPU**). Increase it if GPU memory is sufficient.","locale":"en"},"6351":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-88","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-job-queues","content":"With `cpu` as the device, these become CPU jobs, with at most half the CPU core count running concurrently.","locale":"en"},"6352":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-89","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-job-queues","content":"When an experiment's **GPUs** setting is `cpu` (new experiments take the default from `training.gpus`), training also runs as a CPU job.","locale":"en"},"6353":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-90","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#gpu-job-queues","content":"Previews (converting only the first few seconds) have higher priority than normal conversion.","locale":"en"},"6354":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-91","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#live-voice-conversion-has-priority","content":"Live voice conversion is most sensitive to latency, so starting it (with a device other than `cpu`) takes the GPU lease:","locale":"en"},"6355":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-92","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#live-voice-conversion-has-priority","content":"GPU jobs already running continue;","locale":"en"},"6356":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-93","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#live-voice-conversion-has-priority","content":"New GPU jobs wait, and their cards show **Run anyway** with the hint **Live is using the GPU. Running both may cause audio glitches.** Click it to start without waiting for live voice conversion to stop;","locale":"en"},"6357":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-94","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#live-voice-conversion-has-priority","content":"When live voice conversion stops, waiting jobs start automatically.","locale":"en"},"6358":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-95","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#live-voice-conversion-has-priority","content":"Command-line jobs (`rvc-next convert`, etc.) run in their own processes and do not participate in a running server's GPU queue. Running a GPU job from the command line on the same computer while the server is doing live voice conversion can also cause audio glitches.","locale":"en"},"6359":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-96","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#live-latency","content":"Live voice conversion processes one \"block\" of audio at a time. Estimated latency (shown on the Live page and by `live check`) is calculated as:","locale":"en"},"6360":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-97","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#live-latency","content":"With the default **Block** of 250 milliseconds and **Crossfade** of 50 milliseconds, one duplex stream gives about 560 milliseconds plus device latency.","locale":"en"},"6361":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-98","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#live-latency","content":"Device latency here is what PortAudio reports, which is often optimistic (MME, DirectSound and Bluetooth devices especially). For the real figure, connect the output to the input and use **Measure latency** on the Live page or `rvc-next live latency`: it plays noise bursts through the same buffers a session uses, finds them in the input by cross-correlation, and adds the converter's own delay. See Measure the real latency.","locale":"en"},"6362":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-99","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#live-latency","content":"To avoid gaps, inference time per block must be well below the block length. The **Load** bar on the Live page shows the ratio of inference time (p95) to block length:","locale":"en"},"6363":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-100","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#live-latency","content":"Above 80% load, the bar turns red and shows **Increase block to \\{ms} ms** (the suggested value targets about 70% load, capped at 1500 milliseconds). Click to apply;","locale":"en"},"6364":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-101","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#live-latency","content":"If **underruns**, **overruns**, or crackling occur, also increase the block length;","locale":"en"},"6365":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-102","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#live-latency","content":"Changing block length, crossfade, or context while running rebuffers without reloading the model.","locale":"en"},"6366":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-103","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#live-latency","content":"This is where CPU and GPU differ most: fast GPU inference allows shorter blocks for lower latency; CPU inference is much slower and usually needs longer blocks (increasing latency). The pm pitch method is also faster than RMVPE. Longer **Context** makes the result smoother but increases computation per block; denoising adds some processing time.","locale":"en"},"6367":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware-104","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-hardware#live-latency","content":"For more on using live voice conversion, see Live.","locale":"en"},"6368":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","type":"page","content":"Migrate from original RVC","breadcrumbs":["RVC Next Documentation","Advanced"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","locale":"en"},"6369":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-0","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","content":"RVC Next is a rewrite of Retrieval-based-Voice-Conversion-WebUI (based on commit 81eed5e).","locale":"en"},"6370":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-1","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"1\\. Reuse the original `assets` folder","locale":"en"},"6371":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-2","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#2-use-original-voices-directly","content":"2\\. Use original voices directly","locale":"en"},"6372":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-3","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#3-copy-voices-into-the-voice-library","content":"3\\. Copy voices into the voice library","locale":"en"},"6373":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-4","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#4-import-original-training-experiments","content":"4\\. Import original training experiments","locale":"en"},"6374":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-5","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Behavior differences from the original","locale":"en"},"6375":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-6","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#bit-identical-behavior","content":"Bit-identical behavior","locale":"en"},"6376":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-7","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","content":"RVC Next is a rewrite of Retrieval-based-Voice-Conversion-WebUI (based on commit `81eed5e`). The original model file format is unchanged: voices, indexes, base models, and experiments from the original can be used directly, and voices trained with RVC Next use the same format. This page explains how to reuse an existing original RVC installation and how the two differ in behavior.","locale":"en"},"6377":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-8","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","content":"Migration does not modify any files in the original installation, so you can use both side by side.","locale":"en"},"6378":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-9","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"RVC Next's asset folder has the same structure as the original `assets/`:","locale":"en"},"6379":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-10","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"Asset","locale":"en"},"6380":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-11","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"Path","locale":"en"},"6381":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-12","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"HuBERT","locale":"en"},"6382":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-13","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"`hubert_base/`","locale":"en"},"6383":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-14","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"RMVPE","locale":"en"},"6384":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-15","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"`rmvpe/rmvpe.pt` (`rmvpe/rmvpe.onnx` for DirectML)","locale":"en"},"6385":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-16","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"Base models","locale":"en"},"6386":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-17","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"`pretrained/`, `pretrained_v2/` (e.g. `f0G40k.pth`, `f0D40k.pth`)","locale":"en"},"6387":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-18","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"Separation models","locale":"en"},"6388":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-19","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"`pymss_weights/`","locale":"en"},"6389":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-20","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"FCPE","locale":"en"},"6390":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-21","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"`fcpe/fcpe_c_v001.pt` (bundled with torchfcpe in the original; RVC Next needs a separate download)","locale":"en"},"6391":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-22","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"Point `paths.assets_dir` to the original `assets` folder to avoid downloading these files again:","locale":"en"},"6392":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-23","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"Under **Settings** › **Storage** › **Folders**, set **Assets** to the path of the original RVC `assets` folder.","locale":"en"},"6393":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-24","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#1-reuse-the-original-assets-folder","content":"Once you point to the original `assets` folder, assets downloaded later in RVC Next (such as FCPE or missing base models) are also written there.","locale":"en"},"6394":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-25","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#2-use-original-voices-directly","content":"After adding the original installation as an \"Original RVC installation\" (`paths.legacy_roots` in settings), its voices are **read directly without copying**:","locale":"en"},"6395":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-26","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#2-use-original-voices-directly","content":"Voices come from `<RVC>/assets/weights/*.pth`;","locale":"en"},"6396":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-27","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#2-use-original-voices-directly","content":"Indexes are searched for in `<RVC>/assets/indices` and `<RVC>/logs`. The original `get_index_path_from_model` scoring rules (experiment name, speaker, and old/new) recommend an `added_*.index` for each voice; indexes with `trained` in their names are excluded.","locale":"en"},"6397":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-28","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#2-use-original-voices-directly","content":"In the Web UI: at the bottom of **Models** › **Voices**, find **Original RVC installs**, click **Add an RVC install**, and choose the RVC root directory (it must contain an `assets` folder). Then:","locale":"en"},"6398":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-29","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#2-use-original-voices-directly","content":"These voices are marked **legacy**, appear in all voice pickers, and can be used directly for conversion and live voice conversion.","locale":"en"},"6399":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-30","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#2-use-original-voices-directly","content":"**Scan**: rereads `assets/weights` and recommends indexes using the original rules for voices whose indexes have not yet been chosen. Voices whose indexes you have manually added or removed keep your choices.","locale":"en"},"6400":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-31","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#2-use-original-voices-directly","content":"**Import all**: copies all voices from this installation into the RVC Next voice library.","locale":"en"},"6401":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-32","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#2-use-original-voices-directly","content":"Recommendations and subsequent edits (renaming, speaker names, changing indexes, hiding) are saved in `legacy-voices.json` in the data directory; the original folder is never written to. Uploaded indexes attached to legacy voices are copied to `models/attached-indexes/<voice>/`; indexes supplied as server paths are recorded by path only. Deleting a legacy voice only hides it (`rvc-next model list --all` still lists it), without changing files in the RVC installation.","locale":"en"},"6402":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-33","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#2-use-original-voices-directly","content":"To add an installation from the command line, write the same settings. The next command or server start reads them and recommends indexes:","locale":"en"},"6403":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-34","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#2-use-original-voices-directly","content":"By default, the Web UI allows browsing files in added original RVC installations (see the browsing scope in Remote access and security).","locale":"en"},"6404":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-35","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#3-copy-voices-into-the-voice-library","content":"Import voices if you want them to be independent of the original installation (for example, if you plan to delete it). Each file is identified by content during import; `.pth` and `.index` file names do not need to match:","locale":"en"},"6405":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-36","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#3-copy-voices-into-the-voice-library","content":"Index width determines the compatible version (256 dimensions for v1, 768 for v2); incompatible versions are never paired;","locale":"en"},"6406":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-37","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#3-copy-voices-into-the-voice-library","content":"If an import contains only one compatible voice, pairing is automatic. With multiple voices, scores use experiment names, name similarity, and whether files share a folder. If the match is uncertain, you choose (the Web UI opens **Review the import**; the command line prints the plan and exits with code 3).","locale":"en"},"6407":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-38","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#3-copy-voices-into-the-voice-library","content":"Drop `.pth`, `.index`, or `.zip` files into the import area in **Models** › **Voices**. For legacy voices, you can also click **Import all** under **Original RVC installs**.","locale":"en"},"6408":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-39","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#3-copy-voices-into-the-voice-library","content":"Training `G_*.pth`/`D_*.pth` files are imported as **Base models** by default (you can then select them in experiment settings). To turn a G checkpoint directly into a voice, add `--extract` or extract it in **Models** › **Tools**.","locale":"en"},"6409":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-40","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#4-import-original-training-experiments","content":"An original experiment folder, `logs/<name>`, can be copied in for continued use:","locale":"en"},"6410":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-41","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#4-import-original-training-experiments","content":"On **Train**, click **Import experiment** and enter the **Original experiment folder (logs/\\<name>)**.","locale":"en"},"6411":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-42","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#4-import-original-training-experiments","content":"The folder must contain `0_gt_wavs` or `config.json`. The `eval` folder is skipped when copying. Stages whose outputs already exist (slice, pitch, features, fit, index) are marked complete; you can then run unfinished stages or increase the epoch count to continue training. If an experiment folder has no `experiment.json`, RVC Next reads it as an original experiment.","locale":"en"},"6412":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-43","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"These changes are intentional (all other numerical calculations correspond line by line to the original):","locale":"en"},"6413":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-44","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Area","locale":"en"},"6414":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-45","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Original","locale":"en"},"6415":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-46","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"RVC Next","locale":"en"},"6416":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-47","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Slicing","locale":"en"},"6417":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-48","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Every slice except the last loses its tail (a regression in the original)","locale":"en"},"6418":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-49","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Keeps every slice's tail","locale":"en"},"6419":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-50","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Speaker in live voice conversion","locale":"en"},"6420":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-51","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Fixed to speaker 0","locale":"en"},"6421":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-52","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Uses the selected speaker; multi-speaker voices work with live voice conversion","locale":"en"},"6422":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-53","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Index rebuilding","locale":"en"},"6423":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-54","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Skipped whenever `added_*.index` exists","locale":"en"},"6424":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-55","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Records feature file fingerprints (name, size, modification time) and rebuilds when they change","locale":"en"},"6425":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-56","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Index loading","locale":"en"},"6426":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-57","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Rereads the index for every file","locale":"en"},"6427":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-58","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Caches by path and modification time; loads only once","locale":"en"},"6428":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-59","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Pitch methods","locale":"en"},"6429":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-60","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Only pm and rmvpe on the command line","locale":"en"},"6430":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-61","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Conversion, the command line, and live voice conversion all support pm, rmvpe, fcpe, and also CREPE (full and tiny) and SwiftF0 (training still uses pm and rmvpe)","locale":"en"},"6431":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-62","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Formant","locale":"en"},"6432":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-63","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Supported only in live voice conversion","locale":"en"},"6433":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-64","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Also supported offline, using the live method; a formant value of 0 gives bit-identical results to the original","locale":"en"},"6434":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-65","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Paths","locale":"en"},"6435":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-66","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"The live GUI rejects non-ASCII paths","locale":"en"},"6436":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-67","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Any path accepted by the system","locale":"en"},"6437":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-68","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Live inference","locale":"en"},"6438":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-69","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Runs in the audio callback","locale":"en"},"6439":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-70","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"The callback only moves data; inference runs on a separate thread","locale":"en"},"6440":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-71","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Changing live block length, crossfade, and context","locale":"en"},"6441":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-72","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Requires stopping the audio stream","locale":"en"},"6442":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-73","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Rebuffers without reloading the model","locale":"en"},"6443":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-74","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#behavior-differences-from-the-original","content":"Single-speaker slicing also processes only top-level audio files in the dataset folder (matching dataset scanning). A voice is considered multi-speaker based on speaker information in the `.pth`, rather than the number of embedding rows (every trained voice has 109 rows).","locale":"en"},"6444":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-75","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#bit-identical-behavior","content":"Numerical code is ported rather than rewritten and checked against the original with golden tests: outputs are compared on CPU, in fp32, with the same random seed and the same 16 kHz input.","locale":"en"},"6445":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-76","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#bit-identical-behavior","content":"**Offline conversion**: pm and RMVPE are **bit-identical** for v1 40k, v2 40k, v2 40k without pitch guidance, and v2 48k voices, covering speech, silence, and a 70-second clip exceeding the chunking threshold, with pitch shift and speaker IDs. FCPE is also bit-identical.","locale":"en"},"6446":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-77","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#bit-identical-behavior","content":"**Live voice conversion**: the streaming engine is bit-identical block by block to the original `rtrvc.RVC.infer` plus the live GUI callback processing (gate, TorchGate input/output denoising, formant, loudness mixing, SOLA).","locale":"en"},"6447":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate-78","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-migrate#bit-identical-behavior","content":"Offline output may be up to two 10-millisecond frames shorter than the input, due to the original's flooring behavior.","locale":"en"},"6448":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","type":"page","content":"Remote access and security","breadcrumbs":["RVC Next Documentation","Advanced"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","locale":"en"},"6449":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-0","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","content":"The RVC Next server listens only on the local machine by default (127.0.0.1:7868), so only browsers on that computer can open it. This page explains how to allow other devices on the local network to connect and the protections the server applies.","locale":"en"},"6450":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-1","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"Address and port","locale":"en"},"6451":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-2","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#allow-other-computers-to-connect","content":"Allow other computers to connect","locale":"en"},"6452":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-3","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#access-token-optional","content":"Access token (optional)","locale":"en"},"6453":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-4","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#how-to-send-the-token","content":"How to send the token","locale":"en"},"6454":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-5","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"What remote browsers can do","locale":"en"},"6455":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-6","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#browsing-scope","content":"Browsing scope","locale":"en"},"6456":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-7","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#origin-checks","content":"Origin checks","locale":"en"},"6457":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-8","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#live-devices","content":"Live voice conversion: the server's devices or the browser's","locale":"en"},"6458":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-9","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#reverse-proxies","content":"Reverse proxies","locale":"en"},"6459":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-10","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#related-pages","content":"Related pages","locale":"en"},"6460":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-11","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","content":"The server can delete and move files, read audio on the server, and open audio devices. Opening it to other computers needs no access token, but everyone who can connect has these powers: make sure the network is trusted, or add protection of your own such as a firewall, a VPN or a reverse proxy with authentication. The access token is an optional extra layer.","locale":"en"},"6461":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-12","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"Setting","locale":"en"},"6462":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-13","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"Command-line option","locale":"en"},"6463":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-14","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"Default","locale":"en"},"6464":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-15","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"Description","locale":"en"},"6465":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-16","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"`server.host`","locale":"en"},"6466":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-17","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"`--host`","locale":"en"},"6467":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-18","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"`127.0.0.1`","locale":"en"},"6468":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-19","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"Address to bind. `0.0.0.0` listens on all IPv4 interfaces; `::` listens on all IPv6 interfaces. You can also specify the address of one interface","locale":"en"},"6469":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-20","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"`server.port`","locale":"en"},"6470":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-21","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"`--port`","locale":"en"},"6471":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-22","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"`7868`","locale":"en"},"6472":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-23","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"First port to try; `--port 0` lets the system choose a free port","locale":"en"},"6473":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-24","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"`server.strict_port`","locale":"en"},"6474":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-25","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"`--strict-port`","locale":"en"},"6475":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-26","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"Off","locale":"en"},"6476":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-27","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"Fail when the port is in use","locale":"en"},"6477":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-28","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"`server.open_browser`","locale":"en"},"6478":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-29","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"`--open / --no-open`","locale":"en"},"6479":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-30","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"On","locale":"en"},"6480":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-31","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"Open the browser after starting","locale":"en"},"6481":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-32","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#address-and-port","content":"If a port is in use (or reserved by the system), the server tries subsequent ports in order, up to 20, and reports \"Port 7868 is unavailable. Using port 7869.\" in the log. With `strict_port` enabled, it exits immediately with code 3. In the Web UI, these settings are **Server address** and **Port** under **Settings** › **General**. After changing them: **Takes effect the next time the server starts.**","locale":"en"},"6482":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-33","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#allow-other-computers-to-connect","content":"Bind to all interfaces and start:","locale":"en"},"6483":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-34","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#allow-other-computers-to-connect","content":"The server notes in its log that other computers can reach it:","locale":"en"},"6484":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-35","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#allow-other-computers-to-connect","content":"Open `http://<server IP>:7868` in a browser on another device and use it.","locale":"en"},"6485":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-36","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#allow-other-computers-to-connect","content":"No access token is needed: anyone who can reach the address can use every feature. If the network is not fully trusted, add protection of your own (a firewall that lets in only trusted devices, a VPN, or a reverse proxy with authentication), or set the access token below.","locale":"en"},"6486":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-37","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#access-token-optional","content":"An access token is any sufficiently long random string; once set, every browser has to enter it first:","locale":"en"},"6487":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-38","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#access-token-optional","content":"Under **Settings** › **General**, enter an **Access token** and click **Set token**. The adjacent indicator shows **An access token is set** or **No access token**. **Clear token** removes it.","locale":"en"},"6488":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-39","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#access-token-optional","content":"Once it is set, an **Access token** dialog appears when the page loads (\"This server asks for its access token.\"). Enter the token and click **Save**. The browser saves the token in local storage and writes a Cookie named `rvc_next_token` (for audio playback and WebSocket connections), so you do not need to enter it again next time. If the token is incorrect or changes, the dialog reappears.","locale":"en"},"6489":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-40","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#access-token-optional","content":"The token applies to all browsers, **including browsers on the server machine**: after setting a token, opening the Web UI locally also asks for it once. The command line does not use the API and needs no token.","locale":"en"},"6490":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-41","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#access-token-optional","content":"By default the server provides only HTTP. Tokens and audio travel over the network in plaintext, so it is suitable only for a trusted local network; for encryption, give it a certificate (see below) or let a reverse proxy provide HTTPS. You may need to allow the port through the operating system's firewall.","locale":"en"},"6491":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-42","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#how-to-send-the-token","content":"Once a token is set, every request under `/api/`, `/ws/`, `/openapi.json`, and `/docs` must carry it, using any of these three methods:","locale":"en"},"6492":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-43","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#how-to-send-the-token","content":"Request header `Authorization: Bearer <token>`;","locale":"en"},"6493":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-44","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#how-to-send-the-token","content":"Cookie `rvc_next_token=<token>`;","locale":"en"},"6494":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-45","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#how-to-send-the-token","content":"Query parameter `?token=<token>`.","locale":"en"},"6495":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-46","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#how-to-send-the-token","content":"Only the health check `/api/v1/app/health` (which tells the page whether a token is required) and the Web UI's static pages do not require a token. Tokens are compared in constant time. Example API call from a script:","locale":"en"},"6496":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-47","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#how-to-send-the-token","content":"Token changes take effect immediately, without restarting the server.","locale":"en"},"6497":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-48","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"When a token is set, all requests must pass the token check first. The server then (or right away, without a token) determines which operations to allow based on whether the request comes from the local machine:","locale":"en"},"6498":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-49","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"Operation","locale":"en"},"6499":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-50","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"Local browser","locale":"en"},"6500":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-51","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"Other computer","locale":"en"},"6501":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-52","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"Upload audio, convert, separate, train, live voice conversion, manage models, change settings","locale":"en"},"6502":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-53","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"Allowed","locale":"en"},"6503":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-54","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"Allowed","locale":"en"},"6504":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-55","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"Upload a training dataset (**Add audio from this computer**), download a voice (**Download**) or results","locale":"en"},"6505":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-56","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"Allowed","locale":"en"},"6506":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-57","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"Allowed: nothing needs a folder on the server","locale":"en"},"6507":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-58","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"Browse server files (**Server files**), import models by server path, or specify a dataset","locale":"en"},"6508":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-59","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"Allowed","locale":"en"},"6509":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-60","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"Allowed, but only within the browsing scope","locale":"en"},"6510":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-61","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"**Show in folder** (open in the server's file manager)","locale":"en"},"6511":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-62","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"Allowed","locale":"en"},"6512":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-63","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"Not allowed (\"This works only in a browser on the server machine\")","locale":"en"},"6513":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-64","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#what-remote-browsers-can-do","content":"Anyone who can open the Web UI (with a token set, anyone holding it) can change all settings (including browsing scope and folder locations) and delete voices and experiments. Let only people you trust reach the server.","locale":"en"},"6514":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-65","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#browsing-scope","content":"When browsing or using server files, the Web UI (including local browsers) can access only folders in `paths.browse_roots`. Paths outside this scope are rejected (`invalid_path`). By default (an empty list), the scope includes:","locale":"en"},"6515":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-66","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#browsing-scope","content":"The user's home directory;","locale":"en"},"6516":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-67","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#browsing-scope","content":"Added original RVC installations;","locale":"en"},"6517":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-68","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#browsing-scope","content":"The data directory;","locale":"en"},"6518":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-69","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#browsing-scope","content":"All drives (`C:\\`, `D:\\`, etc. on Windows), or mounted volumes (`/Volumes/*` on macOS; `/media/<user>/*`, `/run/media/<user>/*`, `/mnt/*` on Linux). The system root `/` is excluded by default.","locale":"en"},"6519":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-70","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#browsing-scope","content":"When allowing other computers to connect, narrow the scope to the folders you need:","locale":"en"},"6520":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-71","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#browsing-scope","content":"Or enter one folder per line in **Folders the browser may open** under **Settings** › **Storage**. Once set, only these folders can be browsed.","locale":"en"},"6521":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-72","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#browsing-scope","content":"The command line is not restricted by the browsing scope: someone running commands on the server already has access to these files.","locale":"en"},"6522":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-73","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#origin-checks","content":"To prevent other websites from using your browser to operate the server, the server applies two checks to each request:","locale":"en"},"6523":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-74","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#origin-checks","content":"**Host check**: when listening only locally, the request's `Host` must be `localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, `::1`, or the bound address; otherwise the server returns 400 (`bad_host`). This prevents DNS rebinding attacks. The check is skipped when bound to a non-local address.","locale":"en"},"6524":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-75","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#origin-checks","content":"**Origin check**: for requests that modify data (non-GET/HEAD/OPTIONS) and WebSocket connections, an `Origin`, if present, must match the page's own address or appear in `server.allowed_origins`; otherwise the server returns 403 (`bad_origin`). Cross-site requests without an `Origin` are also rejected.","locale":"en"},"6525":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-76","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#origin-checks","content":"If you call the API from another page or tool (such as your own frontend), add its origin to `server.allowed_origins`. The server also enables CORS for these origins (CORS is configured at startup, so changes require a restart):","locale":"en"},"6526":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-77","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#live-devices","content":"Live voice conversion runs on **the server computer**. With **Server devices** (the default), microphone input, converted output and monitoring use that computer's audio devices, not the device on which you opened the page. The device panel title names the host (**Audio devices on \\<host>**); remote sessions also show **These devices belong to the server machine, not this browser.** The command `rvc-next live devices` likewise lists devices on the server machine.","locale":"en"},"6527":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-78","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#live-devices","content":"With **This browser** at the top of the Live page, the microphone of the phone or computer you opened the page on goes to the server, and the converted voice plays there (Use this browser's microphone). Browsers allow a page to use the microphone only over **HTTPS**, or on the server itself (`localhost`). For other computers, give the server a certificate:","locale":"en"},"6528":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-79","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#live-devices","content":"A self-signed certificate works after the browser's warning is accepted (`openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout key.pem -out cert.pem -days 365 -subj /CN=my-pc`). A reverse proxy that terminates HTTPS works too; it must forward the WebSocket at `/api/v1/live/browser-audio` as well as those under `/ws/`.","locale":"en"},"6529":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-80","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#reverse-proxies","content":"The code has no specific support for running behind a reverse proxy and no configurable path prefix. If you do use one, account for the checks above:","locale":"en"},"6530":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-81","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#reverse-proxies","content":"When the server listens only locally, the forwarded `Host` must be `localhost` or `127.0.0.1` (otherwise 400 `bad_host`); a program embedding rvc-next can allow a proxy's or a tunnel's public host name with `extra_hosts` / `allow_host()` (Embedding);","locale":"en"},"6531":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-82","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#reverse-proxies","content":"The browser sends the proxy's address as its `Origin`, which differs from the forwarded `Host`; add it to `server.allowed_origins`;","locale":"en"},"6532":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-83","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#reverse-proxies","content":"Forward WebSocket connections under `/ws/`, and `/api/v1/live/browser-audio` (browser audio);","locale":"en"},"6533":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-84","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#reverse-proxies","content":"The server treats a request as local only when it comes from a loopback address and its `Host` is a local name (`localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, `::1`). If the proxy and server are on the same computer and the proxy rewrites `Host` to `localhost`, all visitors are treated as local; a proxy or tunnel that keeps its public host name is not. When exposing the server to the internet, add authentication at the proxy or set an access token.","locale":"en"},"6534":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-85","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#related-pages","content":"Configuration and environment variables: full descriptions of `server` and `paths` settings.","locale":"en"},"6535":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-86","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#related-pages","content":"Settings: the Web UI settings page.","locale":"en"},"6536":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote-87","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced-remote","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced-remote#related-pages","content":"Live","locale":"en"},"6537":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced","type":"page","content":"Overview","breadcrumbs":["RVC Next Documentation","Advanced"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/advanced","locale":"en"},"6538":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-0","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced","content":"This section is for users who can already use RVC Next and want to customize it further: reuse original RVC files, access the server from other computers, tune performance for their GPU, and troubleshoot common issues.","locale":"en"},"6539":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-1","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced","content":"Reuse the original RVC `assets` folder, read original voices and indexes directly, import voices and experiments, and learn which behaviors differ from the original and which are bit-identical.","locale":"en"},"6540":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-2","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced","content":"Binding address and port, access tokens, what remote browsers can and cannot do, origin checks, and why live voice conversion always uses the server's audio devices.","locale":"en"},"6541":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-3","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced","content":"Start the server and web UI from your own Python program: data and configuration in separate folders, pinned settings, a path prefix, and mounting into an existing FastAPI app.","locale":"en"},"6542":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-4","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced","content":"Device and precision selection rules, CUDA Graph, DirectML and MPS, GPU memory management and **Free GPU memory**, out-of-memory handling, GPU job queues, and live voice conversion latency and block length.","locale":"en"},"6543":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-5","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced","content":"Answers to questions about missing assets, slow CPU conversion, missing devices or crackling in live voice conversion, ports in use, other drives not appearing, where results are saved, and more.","locale":"en"},"6544":{"id":"/en/docs/advanced-6","page_id":"/en/docs/advanced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/advanced","content":"For command-line settings and environment variables, see Configuration and environment variables; for settings in the interface, see Settings.","locale":"en"},"6545":{"id":"/en/docs/cli-commands","page_id"