` is neither an RVC small model nor a G checkpoint","locale":"en"},"8279":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-178","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"`AssetMissingError`","locale":"en"},"8280":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-179","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"`asset_missing`","locale":"en"},"8281":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-180","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"409","locale":"en"},"8282":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-181","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"7","locale":"en"},"8283":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-182","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"HuBERT, RMVPE, FCPE or base model not installed; `detail.assets` lists asset ids used by the UI to offer downloads","locale":"en"},"8284":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-183","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"`DeviceError`","locale":"en"},"8285":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-184","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"`device_unavailable`","locale":"en"},"8286":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-185","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"409","locale":"en"},"8287":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-186","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"8","locale":"en"},"8288":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-187","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"Audio device missing, busy or rejects the format; `detail.reason` is `missing` / `busy` / `format` / `permission`","locale":"en"},"8289":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-188","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"`ComputeError`","locale":"en"},"8290":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-189","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"`compute_unavailable`","locale":"en"},"8291":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-190","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"503","locale":"en"},"8292":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-191","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"9","locale":"en"},"8293":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-192","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"GPU unavailable or out of memory (`detail.reason = \"out_of_memory\"`)","locale":"en"},"8294":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-193","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"`RvcNextError` (base class)","locale":"en"},"8295":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-194","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"`internal_error`","locale":"en"},"8296":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-195","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"500","locale":"en"},"8297":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-196","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"1","locale":"en"},"8298":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-197","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"Other cases","locale":"en"},"8299":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-198","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"The API layer also defines `NotLocalError` (`not_local`, 403) for operations such as “Show in folder” that only make sense for a browser on the server machine.","locale":"en"},"8300":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-199","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"Errors reach this table from three sources:","locale":"en"},"8301":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-200","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"**Engine exceptions** (`engine/errors.py`: `MissingAssetError`, `ModelFormatError`, `AudioError`, `Cancelled`) are converted by `map_engine_error()` in `core/engine_errors.py`; the engine never imports core. Out-of-memory errors are identified by `engine/runtime.is_oom()` (`torch.OutOfMemoryError`, `AcceleratorError` with code 2, MPS / DirectML error messages).","locale":"en"},"8302":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-201","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"**Worker `error` events** are reconstructed by `worker_error()`.","locale":"en"},"8303":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-202","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors-and-exit-codes","content":"**Live worker** `State.error` uses the same `code` values.","locale":"en"},"8304":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-203","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#settings","content":"`core/settings/models.py` is a tree of `Record` models rooted at `Settings`, with groups `server`, `paths`, `compute`, `downloads`, `convert`, `separation`, `training`, `live`. `SettingsService` owns `settings.toml` in the data directory and merges values in this order (later values override earlier ones):","locale":"en"},"8305":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-204","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#settings","content":"Environment variables use `__` to separate levels, for example `RVC_NEXT_SERVER__PORT=8000`, `RVC_NEXT_COMPUTE__DEVICE=cpu`; values are parsed as JSON if possible, otherwise as strings, with `null` / `none` meaning empty. Environment variables and overrides are never written back to the file. `RVC_NEXT_DATA_DIR` is not a setting: it determines the data directory itself.","locale":"en"},"8306":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-205","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#settings","content":"`update()` deep-merges the patch, validates, writes atomically (write `.toml.tmp`, then replace) and notifies listeners, allowing a persistent server to follow settings changes without restarting. TOML has no null, so keys with `None` values are omitted when saving.","locale":"en"},"8307":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-206","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#settings","content":"`config set` and the API's `PATCH /settings` share the same dotted-path logic.","locale":"en"},"8308":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-207","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#settings","content":"The public `SettingsView` excludes the access token, exposing only `access_token_configured: bool`; `env_overrides` lists names fixed by environment variables, which the UI uses to display “Set by an environment variable”.","locale":"en"},"8309":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-208","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#settings","content":"Empty directory settings mean “inside the data directory”: `SettingsService.models_dir`, `experiments_dir`, `outputs_dir`, `assets_dir` resolve them. `paths.assets_dir` can point directly to an original RVC `assets/` directory to avoid duplicate downloads.","locale":"en"},"8310":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-209","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#settings","content":"The `live` group (devices, stream parameters, last voice and parameters) lives in settings rather than client state because devices belong to the server machine and the CLI's `live run` needs the same values. Pure UI preferences (theme, language, last page) are client state. See Configuration and environment variables for each group's fields.","locale":"en"},"8311":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-210","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#events","content":"`core/events/models.py` defines `EventBase(Record)`, with each subclass named by its class attribute `__event_name__`; `EventBase.get_events()` collects all concrete subclasses. The API adds their JSON schemas to OpenAPI, so frontend event types are generated too. Current events:","locale":"en"},"8312":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-211","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#events","content":"`job_updated`, `job_log`, `jobs_cleared`, `outputs_added`, `outputs_removed`, `models_changed`, `presets_changed`, `assets_changed`, `experiment_changed`, `train_metrics`, `live_state`, `live_stats`, `devices_changed`, `compute_changed`, `settings_changed`.","locale":"en"},"8313":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-212","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#events","content":"Conventions:","locale":"en"},"8314":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-213","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#events","content":"**Events carry snapshots**, rather than live objects being modified by worker threads: `job_updated` carries a copy of the entire `Job`.","locale":"en"},"8315":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-214","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#events","content":"**Throttle at the source**: job progress is limited to 4 updates per second per job, and logs are batched (at most 50 lines per batch; the UI fills gaps through REST using byte offsets).","locale":"en"},"8316":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-215","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#events","content":"`LocalEventBus` calls handlers **synchronously on the publisher's thread**; handler exceptions are logged without propagating. Subscribers that need to run elsewhere hand off themselves: `SocketBridge` in `api/sockets.py` uses `loop.call_soon_threadsafe` to place events in an asyncio queue, then a task calls `sio.emit(event.__event_name__, event.model_dump(mode=\"json\"))`. CLI progress bars (`cli/progress.py`) subscribe to the same events.","locale":"en"},"8317":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-216","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#events","content":"See API and events for payload details.","locale":"en"},"8318":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-217","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#database","content":"`core/db/database.py`: one shared `sqlite3` connection with an `RLock`; WAL mode, foreign keys enabled, `synchronous=NORMAL`, `busy_timeout=5000` (the CLI reads the same file while the server writes it; for example, `rvc-next jobs list` can see the running server's jobs). Migrations are SQL scripts in the `MIGRATIONS` list: **append only; never change published entries**. `PRAGMA user_version` records how many have run. There are currently two: the first creates `voice_models`, `presets`, `jobs`, `outputs`, `audio_files`, `client_state`; the second adds `catalog_id` to `voice_models` (marking voices downloaded from the demo voice catalog).","locale":"en"},"8319":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-218","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#database","content":"Database contents have two roles:","locale":"en"},"8320":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-219","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#database","content":"**Cache**: `voice_models` can be fully rebuilt from the `models/` directory and legacy RVC installation directories (`models.rescan()`).","locale":"en"},"8321":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-220","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#database","content":"**Record**: job history and output provenance (inputs, voice, parameters).","locale":"en"},"8322":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-221","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#database","content":"Deleting the database loses job history, output provenance, custom presets (defaults are recreated) and the server-side copy of UI preferences; the voice library is rebuilt from disk. Experiments, base models, separation models and unassigned indexes are not in the database at all: they are directories with JSON sidecars, scanned on every listing, so they cannot disagree with the files on disk.","locale":"en"},"8323":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-222","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#data-directory","content":"`default_data_dir()` in `core/paths.py` chooses `RVC_NEXT_DATA_DIR` first, then `%APPDATA%\\rvc-next` on Windows, `~/Library/Application Support/rvc-next` on macOS, and `$XDG_DATA_HOME/rvc-next` on other platforms (default `~/.local/share/rvc-next`).","locale":"en"},"8324":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-223","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#data-directory","content":"`server.json` has another purpose: at startup, `JobService` marks jobs still active as `interrupted`, but skips this if `server.json` shows that another server is using the data directory, avoiding misclassifying that server's jobs as interrupted.","locale":"en"},"8325":{"id":"/en/docs/development-assets","page_id":"/en/docs/development-assets","type":"page","content":"Assets and model repositories","breadcrumbs":["RVC Next Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-assets","locale":"en"},"8326":{"id":"/en/docs/development-assets-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-assets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-assets","content":"Assets are model files RVC Next needs at runtime but does not distribute with its Python package: HuBERT, RMVPE (including ONNX), FCPE, CREPE, training base models, and vocal separation weights.","locale":"en"},"8327":{"id":"/en/docs/development-assets-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-assets","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-assets#the-asset-catalog-catalogjson","content":"The asset catalog: `catalog.json`","locale":"en"},"8328":{"id":"/en/docs/development-assets-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-assets","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-assets#current-assets","content":"Current assets","locale":"en"},"8329":{"id":"/en/docs/development-assets-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-assets","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-assets#two-download-repositories","content":"Two download repositories","locale":"en"},"8330":{"id":"/en/docs/development-assets-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-assets","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-assets#rvc-model-layout","content":"rvc-model layout","locale":"en"},"8331":{"id":"/en/docs/development-assets-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-assets","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-assets#downloads-resume-and-verification","content":"Downloads, resume, and verification","locale":"en"},"8332":{"id":"/en/docs/development-assets-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-assets","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-assets#status","content":"Status","locale":"en"},"8333":{"id":"/en/docs/development-assets-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-assets","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-assets#demo-voices","content":"Demo voices","locale":"en"},"8334":{"id":"/en/docs/development-assets-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-assets","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-assets#models-found-only-in-official-distributions","content":"Models found only in official distributions","locale":"en"},"8335":{"id":"/en/docs/development-assets-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-assets","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-assets#adding-or-updating-assets","content":"Adding or updating assets","locale":"en"},"8336":{"id":"/en/docs/development-assets-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-assets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-assets","content":"Assets are model files RVC Next needs at runtime but does not distribute with its Python package: HuBERT, RMVPE (including ONNX), FCPE, CREPE, training base models, and vocal separation weights. A set of demo voices is also available to download. This page describes the `catalog.json` asset catalog format, the two download repositories, download and verification implementation, and how to add or update assets. See Core services for how other core services call `AssetService`.","locale":"en"},"8337":{"id":"/en/docs/development-assets-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-assets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-assets#the-asset-catalog-catalogjson","content":"`rvc_next/core/assets/catalog.json` is distributed as package data (`package-data` in `pyproject.toml`), read by `core/assets/service.py:load_catalog()`, and validated into records from `core/assets/models.py`. It has three top-level keys:","locale":"en"},"8338":{"id":"/en/docs/development-assets-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-assets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-assets#the-asset-catalog-catalogjson","content":"Record","locale":"en"},"8339":{"id":"/en/docs/development-assets-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-assets","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-assets#the-asset-catalog-catalogjson","content":"Fields","locale":"en"},"8340":{"id":"/en/docs/development-as