e records an input “fingerprint.” After saving changes, affected completed stages are marked **Out of date**, with the message **Inputs changed since this stage ran.**"],[10910,"Each device takes a full row; its menu opens as wide as the row, and long names wrap rather than being cut off. Under each name is a second line: the recommended driver, the channel count and the default sample rate."],[9670,"Each entry point builds its own engine from shared `infer/` code; the live DSP chain exists in three copies in `realtime_gui.py`, `infer/rtrvc.py`, and the VST worker, F0 postprocessing in `pipeline`, `rtrvc`, and `extract_f0`, and PyMSS is both bundled and installed as a pip package (`tools/pymss_webui.py` puts `tools/` first on `sys.path`, selecting the bundled version). rvc-next consolidates these into one copy each."],[8194,"Each feature"],[11773,"Each field shows **Default: \\{path}** below it. Leave it empty to use the default location within the data folder."],[9410,"Each group is a physical device, with `variants` sorted by driver rank and the recommended driver first. Ranks (`HOST_API_RANKS`, lower is better): Windows ASIO → WASAPI → WDM-KS → DirectSound → MME; macOS Core Audio; Linux JACK → ALSA → Pulse → OSS."],[10915,"Each menu item represents one **physical device**, showing its full name. On Windows, the same microphone can appear under several drivers, such as MME, DirectSound and WASAPI. rvc-next combines these into one item and automatically selects the recommended driver (in order of preference: ASIO, WASAPI, WDM-KS, DirectSound, MME on Windows; JACK, ALSA, PulseAudio on Linux; the PortAudio most Linux distributions ship has no PulseAudio driver, so PulseAudio and PipeWire appear as the ALSA devices **pulse**, **pipewire** and **default**)."],[10723,"Each name is followed by the driver's name in brackets, for example *(VB-Audio Voicemeeter VAIO)*. After an update from an older version, choose the devices again in every program that used them."],[8144,"